<!--
    funcboundflow: bound a function's fall-through at the entry of another known
    function.  A kuna FunctionSymbol is an entry address with no extent and CALL is
    fall-through, so a function whose last act is a `call` to a routine kuna cannot
    prove no-return (here the local `fatal` spin-loop; in a stripped static binary
    the unnamed `exit`/`abort`/`__stack_chk_fail` bodies and app `die()`/`throw`
    wrappers) runs its flow through the compiler's inter-function alignment padding
    straight into the NEXT function's entry and decodes THAT function's body into
    itself.  Stage model: S2 flow classification (`process_instruction` fall-through).

    Layout (linked flat):
      caller   @ 0x100040 : if (a0) return a0 + 1; else fatal();   // last op is `call fatal`
      neighbor @ 0x100060 : return a0 - 5;                          // distinctive body
      fatal    @ 0x100070 : for(;;);                                // no-return spin (unproven)
    caller's `call fatal` at 0x10004e has no trailing `ret`; the 0x100053..0x100060
    alignment padding falls through into neighbor's entry (0x100060).

    Pass 1 (default, funcboundflow on): caller is truncated at the `fatal()` call, so
    neighbor's `a0 - 5` body is NOT decoded into caller.
    Pass 2 (funcboundflow off, the bug): caller absorbs neighbor's body, so the
    `(int4)a0 - 5` return appears inside caller.

    Assert #1 pins the merged-body line to EXACTLY ONE occurrence across both print C
    passes: present in the funcboundflow-off pass (the bug), absent in the default pass
    (the fix). Were the default still merging, the line would appear twice.
    Assert #2 pins `fatal()` to both passes, so the only difference is the truncation.
-->
